We report a measurement of the differential decay rate with respect to the hadron's recoil mass for the process $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$. Based on data collected by the LHCb experiment at $\sqrt{s}= 13\ \mathrm{TeV}$, corresponding to an integrated luminosity of $1.0\ \mathrm{fb}^{-1}$, this analysis relies exclusively on $B$-hadron candidates originating from $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ events where the $B_s^0$ is produced via the $B_s^0ightarrow D_s^{*-}\mu^{+}
u_{\mu}$ decay. The theoretical predictions for the differential decay rate as a function of the $W$-boson mass ($W$) are derived using two distinct helicity formalisms. By comparing these theoretical models with the experimental data, we determine the preferred form of the differential decay rate as a function of $W$.
